  "sourceNote": "No validated instrument sits behind this one, because none exists — the questions were written here, and the research was used to choose which ones to ask rather than to say anything about your answers. What that research supports is modest and worth stating at its real size. In a meta-analysis of partner phubbing (Ni and colleagues, Frontiers in Psychology, 2025), the association between feeling phubbed by a partner and relationship satisfaction was r = −0.22 across 30 samples and 9,040 people — real, small, and drawn almost entirely from cross-sectional data that cannot say which way it runs. A dyadic diary study found the effect attaches to the perception rather than to the behaviour: a partner's own reported phone use predicted nothing, while feeling phubbed predicted lower relationship quality on the day — and that day-level effect did not hold two months later either (Carnelley, Vowels, Stanton, Millings and Hart, Computers in Human Behavior 147, 2023). Sharenting is the one subject here where the person most affected is not the one answering: the reviewed harms are documented — a permanent footprint the child did not choose, image misuse, conflict with the grown child over posts they never agreed to — the studies behind them are small and largely descriptive, and in one survey of 1,460 Czech and Spanish parents, around four in five had posted pictures of their child while only about one in five had asked the child first. For group chats, for what should never be typed at all, and for what people want done with their accounts afterwards there is no useful evidence whatever; those questions are here because they get settled by default rather than because anything is known about them. Nothing on this page is scored, inferred, or turned into a number.",

