  /* ── the instruction sheet ─────────────────────────────────────
     Seven headings, on the four channels the spec declares. Not the four
     sections: those are the order the questions are asked in, and a card is
     what somebody checks before they post, answer or put a phone down. Every
     body under these is the reader's own chosen words and nothing composed
     for them.

     Three of the twelve are restrictive multis, and a card prints a title and
     a body and nothing else — no prompt travels with the answer. So the title
     has to carry the direction that the option label cannot: "An apology that
     matters" is a thing that must never arrive as a message, and under a
     heading that did not say so it would read as the opposite. That is why
     `card.spoken` states the prohibition in full, and why the posting card is
     headed "before", which is true of a ceiling on a child's photograph and of
     a list of things that need asking first alike. */
  "card.answering": "How fast I answer, and when work reaches me",
  "card.together": "The phone when we are together",
  "card.photographs": "Intimate photographs",
  "card.posting": "Before anything is posted or repeated",
  "card.open": "What is open, and to whom",
  "card.afterwards": "What is left afterwards",
  "card.spoken": "What must never arrive as a message",

